{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546260-experiences-about-playing-or-dming-fera-chronicles","id":1546260,"title":"Experiences about playing or dming fera chronicles","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-11-21T15:21:06-08:00","id":1546260,"title":"Experiences about playing or dming fera chronicles","content":"Hi there.<br /> <br /> I played before some Fera games but they didn't satisfied me at all. I think the fault was in the DMs part: the first was a bit novice at the time and was more centered about the npcs and their stories, treating the PCs as side characters, and also didn't follow WtA lore completely only when it could fit with his lore (The campaing was set during the Cold War period and both sides of the conflict tried to use the Fera as weapons (including Garou) meanwhile Gaia was more of an afterthought. And the presence of the supernatural was pretty common). The second campaing was a written campaing dmed by a person who dislike written rpgs, because of that the story was all over the place.<br /> <br /> But despite of that, I'm very interested about Fera as a concept, I read some of the changing breeds books and I liked a lot (Mokole and Rokea) and I'm very tempted to DM a Bastet campaing in the future if I find a new group of players. But also I have some questions: 1. Can Changing breeds work separately as WW intended in first place or it gets boring too fast? 2.What are your experiences about Fera campaings (as player and as DM)? 3.What do you prefer: only one type of fera or mixed? What are the advantages and disadvantages of each type of campaing? 4. How do you organize a Fera mixed group? 5.What are your favourite and least favourite Fera?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27558-metalbird","name":"Metalbird","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-21T17:41:18-08:00","id":1546268,"title":"","content":"1) It kinda depends on the Fera picked and the group. Each of the Fera have different issues that can influence what the &quot;ideal&quot; number of players is to cover everything you're trying to do, and not be too overlapping. It also matters if the novelty of the individual Fera wears off or not... which can be very subjective. The Rokea, for example, can either be really fun because of the wildly different perspective, or get boring fast because they're kinda limited thanks to their isolation in the oceans. In an all Rokea game, some people are going to be fine with a fairly repetitive play loop because of the aesthetics, and others are going to quickly feel like every story is just one of two options over and over with slightly different details.<br /> <br /> It's really worth looking at the Fera in question, and both how they operate homogeneous groups, and then looking at what kinds of activities the game presumes the players are going to get up to.<br /> <br /> 2) Generally positive, but most of my Fera games came after years of playing WtA and other WoD games with other experienced players/STs, so we knew how to avoid a lot of the pitfalls the books aren't great at warning you about.<br /> <br /> 3) Like above, it depends. Some of the Fera are very well constructed for homogeneous groups, and others feel very weird to stick into groups of the same kind of shifter. If the group is interested in Fera that aren't well flushed out for extended play with everyone as the same kind of shifter, it's better to use a mixed group with a common cause. If the group is looking at the Fera with more established communities of their own kind, it's easier to run just that splat.<br /> <br /> The advantages of one type of Fera in play is thematic focus. A Nuwisha, a Mokole, and a Rokea are going to struggle to find common cause to work together for one &quot;adventure&quot; let alone an ongoing story. A nest of Najah are meant to operate as a group for extended periods of time like the Garou do, and it works because all the PCs have the same broad focus of what they do. The cons of this is that the Fera just don't have the material as the Garou, and even ones with better social units are still offered a fraction of the story hooks that the Garou get; making it hard to keep the game going.<br /> <br /> The advantage of mixed groups is that everyone can have a more firm niche to occupy in the group much like Garou with Auspices can help ensure a diverse cast of characters. There are also pan-Fera groups like the Hengeyokai and the Ahadi that can be the binding that keeps the group together. As I said, the big disadvantage is that Fera all have different goals and themes they're after, and you end up either diminishing their uniqueness or have a lot of internal conflict between the PCs.<br /> <br /> 4) The multi-Fera factions that already exist are a good start. Beyond that, my preferred approach is some sort of long term common interest the group all agree on. For example, if the PCs are all involved in making sure a buried Wyrm creature stays hidden and bound, you have a common motivation for everyone to base organization around.<br /> <br /> 5) The Nuwisha (for both!). I love the flavor of the were-coyotes, but they're also so superfluous with the Garou's Ragabash Auspice existing. It doesn't help that wolves and coyotes readily interbreed to drive home that treating them as distinct groups is silly. They're great, but should just be a Camp of Garou.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-21T19:40:37-08:00","id":1546271,"title":"","content":"1) I can only speak for my own experiences but I don't think boredom has ever been a problem. Whilst most of my Chronicles have involved more than one type of Fera I've run a number with single types including Rokea, Ananasi and Gumargan.<br /> <br /> Ananasi for instance cover such a vast range of different outlooks and focuses that there is, if anything, too much variety in the types of Chronicles that can be run. Gumargun, and other Mokole, are more focused but still have a wide range of areas of interest and possible and probable Chronicle focuses. The alien environment of the Rokea opens up an entire other world to explore.<br /> <br /> 2) I'd say my mixed Fera Chronicles have probably been some of the most affecting I've run from deeply spiritual quests to bring Songlines back to life, to preventing the destruction of djang sites and preserving ancient knowledge and sacred secrets............all the way up to just absolutely wrecking the entire Japanese whaling fleet for an entire season........and helping an ancient Indigenous Elder Vampire put a rocket up the bulk of the Camarilla and Sabbat on the east coast of Australia<br /> <br /> 3) Whilst I greatly prefer mixed Chronicles, mainly because of the ' resurrecting the Ancient Ahadi-like stucture that used to exist in Australia before the Garou arrived' is a theme thats run through a lot of my Chronicles over the decades. The majority of games I've run have involved Rokea and Gumargan mainly with dashes of Nagah, Corax, Ananasi, Ratkin and occasional Garou........a few of my most recent Chronicles have involved groups of SE Asian Beast Courts members and semi-like minded Hungry Dead working in an uneasy alliance to decolonise regions and destroy mutal dangers. My Gumargan and Rokea wrecking crew ate their way through an entire <i>Chulorviah</i> infestation then went after finning boats in their downtime.<br /> <br /> 4) Ahadi for African Games and Beast Courts for everything East of the Fertile Crescent work pretty well as models, I see no issues with a somewhat looser network existing in the Americas pre-colonisation and managing to survive and re-emerge now or, as mentioned above, The resurrected Ancestors Song Ahadiesque network within Oceania. For more temporary alliances it can simply be that the chronicle is focused on something that is a threat to Everyones interests and resources so it only makes sense to stop the infighting until its put in the ground.<br /> <br /> 5) Favourite? Rokea or Gumargan/Zhong Lung..............<br /> Least Favourite? Not that I have anything against them at all just there hasn't been any place for them in any of my Chronicles- Kitsune.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-21T19:43:23-08:00","id":1546272,"title":"","content":"I find that with Fera games, mixed groups tend to work better. One of the things is, once you open the bag of Fera, each player has their own favorite or not-so-favorite type. Getting everyone on board to all play Bastet or Corax or whatever can be pretty difficult, especially if the ST wants to run a longterm campaign. So allowing each player to play a different type of Fera is, in my experience, the easiest solution that keeps everyone happy. As such games that take place in Africa or Asia tend to be the default for my table, since you have the Ahadi or the Beast Courts to more easily get everyone on the same page. You can run mixed groups in other places, like North or South America, but I find the ST needs to spend more time setting things up to give the players a reason to work together. <br /> <br /> Personally, my favorite was always the Anansi and my least favorite was the Nuwisha (they just always felt kind of pointless).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-22T22:37:43-08:00","id":1546299,"title":"","content":"Talking about the fera I actually saw a neat alternate setting for WtA on a small site Belonging to a Fan Group called Epoch Troupe Games that basically asks a simple question: what if the Garou lost the War of Rage?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The world is in the hands of the fera. Only one breed was driven to extinction in that distant War of Rage, and it was the Wolf-People. However, up until the War of Rage they were one of the most populous and versatile of Gaia\u2019s Changing Ones, and so a major gap has been slowly splintering in the knowledge and skills of the Fera ever since.<br /> <br /> In some ways, things are an improvement. There is no united front of Wyrm-shifters, no Dancers of the Black Spiral. With a force much better suited to more subtle tactics, Pentex is also considerably less powerful, with less fingers in the pies of the human world. However, many other things have been left to rot - many of the other fera cannot access the Umbra with the same ease as the Garou once did, requiring special ritual or even being unable to access without great effort at all, and very few of those with easy access possess the sheer power needed to fight full scale battles there. Perhaps the biggest loss of all were the impressive pack tactics the Garou once wielded; many surviving fera hail from solitary species even without considering the disparity between the different breeds.<br /> <br /> Without the Fangs of Gaia, what are the other cousins to do? </div> </div> </div>Basically the garou went the way of the gondor (totally wiped out besides maybe a few kinfolk) while by contrast most of the extinct breeds made it to the modern nights <i>Mostly </i>intact.<br /> <br /> In the place of the Canon Garou nation is the Accord, an collation of fera whose informally lead by the Bastet (Mainly Khan and Simba though the Swara has been recently stepped up) with the Gurahl as a advinceing role. Said Allance aims to pick up the slack of the extinct garou.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The coalition between the various groups of fera in these modern nights is called The Accord; specifically, this is used to refer to those who work with other breeds at multi-breed caerns and obey their Litany. The Accord is a worldwide alliance, though some fera - mostly the Ananasi and Rokea - decline membership to instead spend time on their own affairs. An Accord \u2018pack\u2019 is called a troop, and they can be bound by a totem spirit regardless of breed of the members. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25848,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25848-mrornx","user":"MrornX","date":". 11-23-2024, 01:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25848-mrornx","name":"MrornX","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"418"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}